# greeter-fake — bring the greeter half up behind upstream greetd's `fakegreet`.
#
# Run as:  sh /bin/greeter-fake        (the kernel has no shebang binfmt)
#
# fakegreet is upstream greetd's protocol test harness: it speaks the full
# greetd IPC on a socket it binds in its own cwd, it sets GREETD_SOCK itself,
# and it answers start_session with Success WITHOUT starting anything. It has no
# PAM, no VT, no session worker, no fork, no /proc/self/exe and no mlockall. So
# it isolates the greeter half — the render environment, DRM, the socket and the
# DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS trap — with none of the daemon's risk surface. If the
# greeter cannot render behind fakegreet, nothing downstream matters.
#
# Its hardcoded credentials are user "user" / password "password" / "7 + 2:" ->
# "9", and it never matches the username cosmic-greeter sends. That is
# deliberate and left alone: an accepted login makes cosmic-greeter exit, which
# makes cosmic-comp exit with it (kiosk mode), which takes the login screen off
# the screen. A rejected login keeps the screen up to be photographed.
set -u

. /bin/greeter-env

# cosmic-greeter picks its role purely by the name getpwuid(getuid()) returns:
# "cosmic-greeter" -> greeter::main(), anything else -> locker::main()
# (cosmic-greeter/src/main.rs). Nothing is copied over /etc/passwd for that any
# more: cosmic-comp's kiosk child is /bin/greeter-launch, which drops to the
# real cosmic-greeter account (uid 990) before exec'ing the greeter, so
# getpwuid returns the name because the process genuinely is that account.

cd "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"
/bin/rm -f greetd.sock 2>/dev/null || true

# ARGUMENT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. cosmic-comp takes its kiosk child from
# env::args().skip(1).next() — the FIRST argument, whatever it is — while its
# flag parser scans every argument and ignores the ones it does not know
# (cosmic-comp/src/lib.rs). So `cosmic-comp --no-xwayland <exec>` spawns
# "--no-xwayland" and dies with ENOENT, while `cosmic-comp <exec> --no-xwayland`
# both disables Xwayland and spawns <exec>. The flag is then also handed to
# cosmic-greeter, whose own argument loop ignores what it does not recognise.
# greeter-launch passes those leftover arguments straight through to the
# greeter. The greeter is staged as cosmic-greeter-login so cosmic-session
# cannot spawn the same binary in its lock-screen role; the login role comes
# from the invoking account, not from the file name.
# --no-xwayland is not optional: smithay's Xwayland-socket bind-error path leaks
# fds -> EMFILE -> a softpipe NULL-transfer crash at first scanout.
echo "GREETER-FAKE: launching fakegreet in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"
exec /bin/fakegreet "/bin/cosmic-comp /bin/greeter-launch --no-xwayland"
